     "page": 1,
     "text": "JOHNSON-JONES CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Kathy Douglas\nDOB: 10/11/1976\nSex: F\nMRN: 603272\n\nAdmit: 10/31/2015\nDischarge: 11/02/2015\n\nAttending Physician: Dr. Phyllis Jones, Hematology\n\n---\n\n**Principal Diagnosis:** Factor V Leiden mutation (venous thromboembolism; Factor V Leiden type)\n\n**Secondary Diagnoses:**\n\n- Upper Respiratory Infection (URI) - diagnosed during a routine follow-up visit on 08/29/2025.\n- Ankle Sprain - treated on 01/22/2025.\n\n**Hospital Course:**\n\nMs. Kathy Douglas, a 49-year-old female, was admitted to Johnson-Jones Clinic on October 2015, for management and monitoring of her known Factor V Leiden mutation, which predisposes her to venous thromboembolism (VTE). \n\nUpon admission, her vital signs were stable with a blood pressure of 141/78 mmHg and a weight of 135 pounds. Physical examination revealed no acute distress. The patient reported mild symptoms of an upper respiratory infection, which was noted during her follow-up visit on August 29, 2025. At that time, her blood pressure was 132/79 mmHg, and her weight was 137 pounds. She was advised symptomatic treatment and hydration.\n\nFurther, she had presented with an ankle sprain on January 22, 2025. At that visit, her blood pressure was 144/81 mmHg, and her weight was 128 pounds. She was treated conservatively with rest, ice, compression, and elevation (RICE protocol).\n\nThroughout her hospital stay, Ms. Douglas remained hemodynamically stable. She did not exhibit any signs of VTE or other complications related to her Factor V Leiden mutation. Her hospital course was uneventful, and she was discharged home in stable condition on November 2, 2015.\n\n**Procedures:**\n\n- None performed during this admission. Routine blood tests and physical examinations were conducted to monitor her condition.\n\n**Discharge Medications:**\n\n- No new medications were prescribed at discharge. The patient was advised continue with her current regimen and lifestyle modifications to manage her condition effectively.\n\n**Follow-Up:**\n\nMs. Douglas was instructed to follow up with her primary care physician and Dr. Phyllis Jones at the hematology clinic for regular monitoring of her Factor V mutation. She was advised to maintain a healthy lifestyle, avoid prolonged immobilization, and seek medical attention if she experiences any symptoms suggestive of VTE, such as unexplained swelling, pain, or redness in the extremities.\n\n**Social History:**\n\n- The patient reports consuming approximately 10 alcoholic drinks per week. She was counseled on the importance of moderate alcohol consumption and the"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "potential risks associated with excessive drinking, especially in the context of her underlying medical condition.\n\n**Review of Systems:**\n\n- **Constitutional:** No fever, chills, or unintended weight loss.\n- **HEENT:** No headaches, vision changes, or hearing loss.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No chest pain, palpitations, or edema.\n- **Respiratory:** No shortness of breath or cough, except for mild symptoms associated with her URI.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** No abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or changes in bowel habits.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports occasional ankle pain following the sprain, otherwise no joint pain or muscle weakness.\n- **Neurological:** No dizziness, syncope, or changes in sensation.\n- **Psychiatric:** No depression, anxiety, or sleep disturbances.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n\n- **General:** Well-nourished, well-developed female in no acute distress.\n- **Vital Signs:** \n - Blood Pressure: 141/78 mmHg (10/31/2015), 132/79 mmHg (08/29/2025), 144/81 mmHg (01/22/2025), 144/89 mmHg (06/17/2024), 143/84 mmHg (04/05/2023).\n - Heart Rate: 72 bpm (regular).\n - Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\n - Temperature: 98.6°F.\n - Weight: 135 pounds (10/31/2015), 137 pounds (08/29/2025), 128 pounds (01/22/2025), 141 pounds (06/17/2024), 130 pounds (04/05/2023).\n- **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic. Pupils equal, round, reactive to light and accommodation. Extraocular movements intact. Tympanic membranes clear. Oropharynx without erythema or exudate.\n- **Neck:** Supple, no lymphadenopathy.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.\n- **Extremities:** No edema, cyanosis, or clubbing. \n- **Neurological:** Cranial nerves II-XII intact. Sensation intact to light touch. Motor strength 5/5 in all extremities. Deep tendon reflexes Z+ bilaterally.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Full range of motion in all joints. Mild tenderness over the malleolus of the right ankle, consistent with the history of sprain.\n\n**Discharge Instructions:**\n\n- Continue with current medications as prescribed.\n- Maintain a healthy diet and regular exercise routine.\n- Avoid prolonged immobilization and seek prompt medical attention for any signs of VTE.\n- Follow up with the primary care physician and hematology clinic as scheduled.\n- Limit alcohol consumption to moderate levels.\n\n**Signature:**\n\nDr. Phyllis Jones\nHematology Specialist\nJohnson-Jones Clinic\n\n---\n\nThis discharge summary provides a comprehensive ove"
    },
    {
     "page": 3,
     "text": "rview of Ms. Kathy Douglas's hospital course, current medical status, and necessary follow-up care.\n"
    }
   ],
